WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.011
Private information retrieval, or PIR

2
00:00:03.083 --> 00:00:05.723
lets a client read one database entry

3
00:00:05.794 --> 00:00:08.220
without the server learning which.

4
00:00:08.570 --> 00:00:10.285
QuarterPIR, by Ghoshal

5
00:00:10.360 --> 00:00:12.224
Zhou and Shi at EUROCRYPT

6
00:00:12.298 --> 00:00:13.715
twenty twenty-four

7
00:00:13.715 --> 00:00:16.250
needs no public-key cryptography.

8
00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:18.612
The client streams the database

9
00:00:18.677 --> 00:00:20.040
once and keeps hints.

10
00:00:20.390 --> 00:00:22.572
Each lookup then needs one short

11
00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:24.550
request and one short reply.

12
00:00:25.130 --> 00:00:27.832
Picture sixteen entries, A to P

13
00:00:27.832 --> 00:00:29.690
in four rows of four.

14
00:00:30.040 --> 00:00:34.180
A hint is one entry per row, say B, G

15
00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:35.160
I and O.

16
00:00:35.510 --> 00:00:37.401
The client stores only the

17
00:00:37.474 --> 00:00:40.310
XOR of their values: the hint's parity.

18
00:00:40.890 --> 00:00:41.839
To read G

19
00:00:41.839 --> 00:00:45.636
the client asks for the parity of B, F

20
00:00:45.636 --> 00:00:46.490
I and O.

21
00:00:46.840 --> 00:00:49.107
G was swapped for a spare entry F

22
00:00:49.107 --> 00:00:50.373
from the same row

23
00:00:50.373 --> 00:00:52.440
whose value the client stored.

24
00:00:52.790 --> 00:00:55.750
XOR the reply with the stored parity

25
00:00:55.750 --> 00:00:58.870
and everything cancels except G XOR F.

26
00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:02.820
One more XOR with F gives G.

27
00:01:03.170 --> 00:01:05.468
The server sees one position per row

28
00:01:05.468 --> 00:01:07.890
and cannot tell which one was swapped.

29
00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:10.000
Each hint serves one lookup

30
00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:12.640
and is then refreshed from a backup hint.

31
00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:15.922
A real database has thousands of rows

32
00:01:15.922 --> 00:01:18.980
so a request cannot name an entry in each.

33
00:01:19.330 --> 00:01:22.210
QuarterPIR puts the rows in groups.

34
00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:25.054
A sixteen-byte key expands into one

35
00:01:25.125 --> 00:01:27.120
position per row of a group.

36
00:01:27.470 --> 00:01:29.669
A request sends one key per group

37
00:01:29.669 --> 00:01:31.286
except the wanted group

38
00:01:31.286 --> 00:01:33.550
which goes as a list of positions.

39
00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:36.379
The reply carries one candidate per group

40
00:01:36.379 --> 00:01:37.914
and every group's parity

41
00:01:37.973 --> 00:01:40.453
because asking for just the one the client

42
00:01:40.512 --> 00:01:42.460
needs would reveal its last read.

43
00:01:42.810 --> 00:01:44.736
At sixty-seven million entries

44
00:01:44.798 --> 00:01:47.531
the reference sends seven hundred thirty-six

45
00:01:47.593 --> 00:01:50.263
bytes up and one thousand twenty-four back

46
00:01:50.263 --> 00:01:52.810
one thousand seven hundred sixty in all.

47
00:01:53.390 --> 00:01:54.990
Pause and predict.

48
00:01:55.540 --> 00:01:58.185
The server's group parities and the client's

49
00:01:58.245 --> 00:02:00.229
own agree in every group but two

50
00:02:00.229 --> 00:02:02.093
the group of the wanted entry

51
00:02:02.153 --> 00:02:04.257
and the group of the entry inserted

52
00:02:04.317 --> 00:02:05.940
at the hint's last refresh.

53
00:02:06.290 --> 00:02:08.370
The client knows both groups.

54
00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:11.280
How few numbers must the server send?

55
00:02:14.630 --> 00:02:15.270
Two.

56
00:02:15.620 --> 00:02:18.180
Two equations give two unknowns.

57
00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:20.767
Of the passing runs

58
00:02:20.767 --> 00:02:23.478
Muse Wonder 1.0 cut the most bytes

59
00:02:23.478 --> 00:02:26.341
with a trick from RAID-6 disk arrays

60
00:02:26.341 --> 00:02:29.580
two checksums can rebuild two lost values.

61
00:02:29.930 --> 00:02:32.204
The server sends only a plain sum and a

62
00:02:32.262 --> 00:02:34.303
weighted sum of its group parities

63
00:02:34.303 --> 00:02:36.810
and the client compares them with its own.

64
00:02:37.160 --> 00:02:38.749
With ordinary numbers

65
00:02:38.821 --> 00:02:41.132
the server's sums are twenty-one

66
00:02:41.205 --> 00:02:42.360
and fifty-seven.

67
00:02:42.710 --> 00:02:45.430
The client's are thirteen and thirty-five.

68
00:02:45.780 --> 00:02:48.365
So the two differences add up to eight

69
00:02:48.432 --> 00:02:50.619
and two times the first plus four

70
00:02:50.685 --> 00:02:52.740
times the second is twenty-two.

71
00:02:53.090 --> 00:02:55.478
So group four differs by three

72
00:02:55.478 --> 00:02:57.250
and group two by five.

73
00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:00.030
The real scheme works with XOR

74
00:03:00.030 --> 00:03:01.520
in a finite field.

75
00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:03.058
By themselves

76
00:03:03.126 --> 00:03:05.453
two sixteen-byte checksums replace

77
00:03:05.521 --> 00:03:07.300
thirty-two group parities.

78
00:03:07.650 --> 00:03:08.444
At full size

79
00:03:08.505 --> 00:03:10.399
the reply falls to five hundred

80
00:03:10.460 --> 00:03:11.498
forty-four bytes

81
00:03:11.559 --> 00:03:13.758
and the whole lookup to one thousand

82
00:03:13.819 --> 00:03:14.980
two hundred eighty

83
00:03:14.980 --> 00:03:16.690
twenty-seven percent fewer.

84
00:03:17.270 --> 00:03:19.501
A new message format would normally

85
00:03:19.565 --> 00:03:21.350
need a new proof of privacy.

86
00:03:21.700 --> 00:03:24.100
The task makes it checkable instead.

87
00:03:24.450 --> 00:03:25.610
For every lookup

88
00:03:25.679 --> 00:03:27.590
the trusted harness computes

89
00:03:27.658 --> 00:03:29.296
the authors' request, R

90
00:03:29.296 --> 00:03:32.094
and passes it alone to the agent's code

91
00:03:32.094 --> 00:03:34.346
in a separate process: no index

92
00:03:34.346 --> 00:03:36.530
no client state, no randomness.

93
00:03:36.880 --> 00:03:39.160
The server receives only the output

94
00:03:39.223 --> 00:03:41.060
a public function of what the

95
00:03:41.123 --> 00:03:42.770
authors' server would see

96
00:03:42.770 --> 00:03:44.480
so it learns nothing more.

97
00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:47.895
A pass needs fewer bytes on every lookup

98
00:03:47.895 --> 00:03:50.660
lower times, and no more client memory.

99
00:03:51.010 --> 00:03:53.490
Eleven of sixteen models passed.

100
00:03:53.840 --> 00:03:55.813
Gemini 4 Argon with Antigravity

101
00:03:55.877 --> 00:03:57.467
had the best final score

102
00:03:57.467 --> 00:03:58.931
sixty-two point eight

103
00:03:58.995 --> 00:04:00.649
against twenty-seven point

104
00:04:00.713 --> 00:04:02.240
three for the reference.

105
00:04:02.590 --> 00:04:03.504
At full size

106
00:04:03.574 --> 00:04:05.752
Kimi K3 sent only seven hundred

107
00:04:05.823 --> 00:04:07.790
thirty-six bytes per lookup.

108
00:04:08.140 --> 00:04:09.982
But it kept six hundred six point

109
00:04:10.038 --> 00:04:11.712
eight megabytes on the client

110
00:04:11.768 --> 00:04:13.163
against eighty-five point

111
00:04:13.219 --> 00:04:15.340
three for the reference, so it failed.

112
00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:18.058
That ends the Cryptography lecture

113
00:04:18.058 --> 00:04:19.280
with three lessons.

114
00:04:19.630 --> 00:04:21.488
The bar can be absolute

115
00:04:21.488 --> 00:04:24.430
no run found all two hundred secrets.

116
00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:27.660
An estimator must be right and fast.

117
00:04:28.010 --> 00:04:30.731
And redesign the message, not just the code

118
00:04:30.793 --> 00:04:33.205
the passing run that cut the most bytes

119
00:04:33.267 --> 00:04:35.370
borrowed an idea from disk arrays.

120
00:04:35.720 --> 00:04:36.793
As an exercise

121
00:04:36.793 --> 00:04:39.154
read entry O with the same hint

122
00:04:39.154 --> 00:04:41.800
and find which spare entry you need.

123
00:04:42.150 --> 00:04:45.430
Next time: Lecture 12, security.
